last night it was round two of the  Republican presidential debate and many  of the presidential hopefuls were  outspoken about Iran and foreign policy  we don't get along with China we don't  get along with the heads of Mexico we  don't get along with anybody and yet at  the same time they rip us left and right  they take advantage of us economically  in every other way if I am elected  president on the very first day in  office I will rip to shreds this  catastrophic Iranian nuclear deal should  we cut up the agreement immediately  that's absurd when you want to know if  they've complied with us now from our  Washington bureaus Bloomberg's world  affairs contributor and us former  defense secretary william cohen bill  thank you so much for joining us this  morning what was your take on the  presidential candidates foreign policy  views last night who do you think was  right on the Iran issue well first they  all deserve credit for standing there  for three hours without a break but  secondly I think you must be careful  what you pledge during any campaign I've  always tried to give advice a be careful  what you promise during a campaign  because number two if you successful you  may have to break the pledge and then  you undermine your own credibility I  think it is wise on the part of those to  say I'm going to think about it very  seriously the first day I'm in office as  to whether I want to take the risk of  tearing something up without consulting  with allies Without Really plumbing the  depths of what this would mean in terms  of the consequences so I think it's one  thing to promise I'll tear it up it's  another thing to say what happens if you  do tear it up what are the consequences  for the United States and our allies I  think they're caution is it's better be  pursued by the candidates rather than  any kind of a bomb best or absolute  ironclad cement cemented pledge to was  and tear it up I think it's a mistake to  do that so I'd say those who urged some  caution on it were the  in my judgment with the winners all  right so Rand Paul seemed to be the only  one on stage urging caution and he said  he wants to keep to the agreement at  least for the time being because he  wants to check to make sure that Iran is  complying with the agreement is this the  kind of thing where we'll be able to  know if they're complying each and every  day or in the first week or first months  I mean isn't a compliance something that  needs to be checked years after the  agreement that is one of the challenges  it was not only Rand Paul I believe that  Jeb Bush also urged some caution as to  John Kasich that they would look at it  very carefully before they would make a  decision as to whether to tear this up  but yes I think initially there will be  an effort on the part of Iran assuming  that it votes to confirm this deal and  that is something is now being debated  within Iran itself but assuming that it  goes forward with this agreement I think  we have to be very careful in the  beginning they're likely to comply as  time goes on the compliance will be less  intense on their part and they may seek  to circumvent it so it's going to  require more intensive effort on the  part of the international inspectors and  that's going to be tough to sustain and  that's why I think the focus ought to be  at this particular point what can we do  now to really shore up the capability of  Saudi Arabia UAE Qatar and the others  especially Israel to make sure that they  are much more secure than they are today  to prevent Iran from using their new  power to present a threat to them so I  think the focus has to be on the  inspections but also what we can do  right now to bolster the capability of  those Gulf states and israel in the  region bill what do you think that of  lindsey graham in the first debate  arguing for a build-up Carly Fiorina and  the second debate arguing for a  significant military buildup have we let  our defense forces down well let me say  this i think it's embarrassing that this  country would be faced with another  government shutdown that sends a signal  to the world that we're not a serious  country  to the extent that we would once again  shut the government of the United States  down doesn't send a signal of strength  or that we're providing leadership  number one number two I think Congress  it's embarrassing that Congress doesn't  have a budget so all of this talk about  we're going to do this with Iran we're  going to confront China we're going to  confront Russia they take a look at your  budget you're going from resolution to  resolution and you really can't plan for  building a strong military unless you  have an agreement up on the hill so I  think there's an embarrassment there  that we don't have a budget and we don't  have really a consensus on the way  forward and that's something that other  countries are looking at they're making  their own assessments of us leadership  when us the US Congress can't reach an  agreement on what needs to be funded  especially our military capability  especially our state Department  especially those programs dealing with  the health and security the people of  this country if we can't agree on that  then we're not gonna be very successful  in having a strong foreign policy  because it won't have the wherewithal to  enforce any kind of strategy that we  have got it thanks very much for that  bill Bloomberg's world affairs  contributor and former defense secretary  bill Cohen
 

